## Reflection
Debugging the broken payment processor was the standout exercise this week — the `balanse` typo was an easy catch, but the double-counted fee and miscounted failure total needed me to actually verify the arithmetic by hand rather than trust that "it ran without crashing" meant it was correct. That distinction between loud bugs and silent bugs is something I'll carry into every script I write from now on. Building the custom exception hierarchy also changed how I think about error handling — attaching `shortfall` or `remaining` directly to the exception object means the caller gets structured data to act on, not just a string to log. 

# Week 4 Reflection

## Q1: Why split one PostgreSQL database into seven AWS services?

FinTrust's original setup ran transactions, sessions, trade documents, and
analytics all on one general-purpose PostgreSQL instance. Splitting this out
is justified because each workload has a genuinely different access pattern:
transactions need ACID guarantees and low-latency writes (RDS Multi-AZ);
sessions need active-active sub-millisecond reads across two Regions
(DynamoDB Global Tables); the regulatory audit trail needs mathematically
provable immutability that no administrator can override (QLDB); and 5-year
BI queries need columnar storage and MPP to scan hundreds of millions of rows
without competing with live transaction writes (Redshift). The operational
cost of this split is real and worth naming honestly: seven services means
seven CloudWatch dashboards to monitor, seven separate IAM policy surfaces to
secure correctly, and seven line items on the AWS bill instead of one. That
cost is accepted because a single RDS instance trying to serve all four
access patterns would degrade every one of them — an analytics query
competing for I/O with a live transaction write is a classic self-inflicted
performance problem.

## Q2: ETL pipeline — SQLite vs RDS Multi-AZ

If two instances of `pipeline.py` ran simultaneously against the same SQLite
file, SQLite's file-level locking would kick in: one process would hold the
write lock while the other blocks or raises a `database is locked` error,
because SQLite only supports a single writer at a time. RDS Multi-AZ does
NOT solve this concurrency problem directly — Multi-AZ exists purely for
Availability Zone-level fault tolerance (a synchronous standby that takes
over automatically if the primary instance or its AZ fails, typically within
60-120 seconds). What actually solves concurrent-write contention at scale is
RDS's underlying engine supporting genuine multi-writer transactions with
row-level locking and MVCC (multi-version concurrency control), plus
connection pooling on the application side. Read-heavy contention is a
separate problem again, solved by Read Replicas, not Multi-AZ — the standby
in a Multi-AZ pair is not even readable. So three different problems, three
different features: single-writer file locking (SQLite's limit) → engine-level
concurrent transactions (RDS/Aurora's design); AZ failure → Multi-AZ; read
scaling → Read Replicas.

## Q3: Benefit of a Python package over a single file

Splitting `pipeline.py` into `fintrust_pipeline/loader.py`, `database.py`,
and `reporter.py` made three things concretely easier, not just "more
organised" in the abstract. First, `validate_row()` in `loader.py` can now be
unit-tested in complete isolation — I can call it with a hand-built dict and
check the return value, with no SQLite connection, no CSV file, and no
report generation involved at all. Second, if FinTrust migrates from SQLite
to RDS PostgreSQL in production, only `database.py` needs to change — the
connection setup and the `?` placeholders become `%s` for `psycopg2` — while
`loader.py` and `reporter.py` don't need to know or care that the underlying
database changed. Third, a CI pipeline can import and test `fintrust_pipeline.loader`
directly without needing to spin up the whole pipeline end-to-end, which
matters once a project has real automated tests running on every commit.

## Q4: Week 4 to Week 5 bridge

The 7-layer database architecture built this week is not yet placed inside a
VPC with correct network isolation — that's Week 5's job. Two specific
configurations that will be needed: first, **RDS in a private subnet** — the
PostgreSQL instance should have no public IP at all, so the only way to reach
it is through the VPC's private routing from the application tier's subnet,
meaning a misconfigured security group on the public internet can never
expose the database directly. Second, a **DynamoDB Gateway Endpoint** — without
one, traffic from an EC2 instance or Lambda function to DynamoDB would route
out over the public internet (even though it never leaves AWS's network
physically) and could be blocked by an overly restrictive NACL; a Gateway
Endpoint keeps that traffic entirely inside the AWS network at the routing
table level, at no additional cost, and is the standard way production
FinTrust workloads reach DynamoDB from inside a VPC.